nformation suggesting extreme nittiness, and you\'re in position (postflop), calling gives you a chance to realize equity.\n\nYet — critical point: your **valid actions** are only [-2, -1], meaning you must either fold or go all-in. But you’ve already put in 200. Opponent went all-in. So to continue, you must call the rest (i.e., add the remaining chips to match their all-in). That action is effectively "call" but represented as -2 because it’s an all-in call.\n\nWait: in this context, -2 means **you go all-in**, which in this case would be calling their all-in. So choosing -2 is calling their all-in bet.\n\nSo the real question is: do you call an all-in with J♣ T♥?\n\nGiven the hand strength, stage (early in the tournament), and chip situation, the best decision is to **call** — because folding here too often allows opponents to exploit you, and you have decent equity.\n\nBut — you have only 19,750, and if opponent shoved over your 200, they likely have a monster. However, since you initiated the raise, their all-in could be a bluff or semi-bluff sometimes.\n\nBut realistically, in a 2-player game, deep-stacked all-ins pre-flop are usually very strong. Still, with ~40% equity on average, calling is reasonable.\n\nBut let’s consider: if you fold, you retain 19,750. If you call and lose, you lose everything. Win, you double up.\n\nGiven that you\'re in the lead cumulatively and this is only hand #2, preserving chips might be wise — but not at the cost of making fundamentally weak folds.\n\nBest approach: **Call the all-in** with this hand, because it has sufficient equity and folding would be overly cautious post-raise.\n\nReason: Jack-10 offsuit has enough equity against a typical all-in range after a 200-raise to justify calling an all-in, especially in a heads-up context where aggression is high and ranges are wide. Folding here would be too tight given the hand’s potential and the early stage of the session.\n\nAnswer: -2'}
